/**

@page page_peano_amr Adaptive mesh refinement

\tableofcontents

Peano expresses dynamic adaptivity as cell requests and realises a structural
change as one rebuild of the tree and the partition followed by one data
transfer. A request names one cell whose neighbourhood refines or whose parent
may erase. Refinement wins over erasure wherever the requests conflict, and
each regrid exposes one additional level on which a criterion can decide again.

# Refinement requests

peano4::RefinementRequest identifies the cell that raises a request by three
fields:

- `refinementControl` selects `Refine` or `Erase`.
- `level` is the cell level counted from the root.
- `latticeIndex` is the cell position on that level's lattice, which holds
  peano4::SubdivisionFactor cells per parent cell along each axis.

A request is trivially copyable and travels between ranks as a byte record.
The order of the requests has no meaning.

# Refinement-plan evaluation

peano4::topology::Tree::updateRefinementPlan() applies the request set through
integer lattice operations.

A refine request covers the named cell and one neighbouring cell in every
direction on the request level. On each coarser level, it covers the cells
containing the two ends of that band. Existing cells in these regions flag
their unrefined corner vertices. Levels finer than the requesting cell do not
participate.

An erase request names the parent cell whose children may disappear. A flagged
vertex is removed only when every existing adjacent cell asks to erase and no
refine request protects that region. Finer flagged vertices veto the removal of
a coarse flag that they still require. All removals are evaluated against one
snapshot, so the request order does not affect the result.

The resulting flag sets are sorted by lattice key. Every rank that receives
the same request set therefore derives the same refinement and ownership
plans and the same rank-boundary order without a structural exchange.

# The regrid

1. Every solver's refinement dispatch writes one command per cell; the
   commands become refinement requests, and exahype2::RefinementRequests
   gives every rank the same request set.
2. The tree adopts the plan and derives the balanced ownership.
3. peano4::Spacetree::regrid() keeps the slot of every surviving cell and
   returns the peano4::Regrid of section @ref peano_partition "the partition":
   prolongations, restrictions, the slots that move to another rank and the
   freed slots.
4. Every solver's `regrid()` restricts and prolongates its cell pools in
   place with the operators of its family, moves the slots that change rank
   through peano4::MigrationExchange, resizes its face pools to the new face
   count and rebuilds them.

During grid construction no solver data exists: a changed plan rebuilds the
tree and the partition directly, and the criterion runs again on the coarser
cells until no request arrives.

# Producing requests

A criterion can request only a cell that exists. A feature that needs several
additional levels produces another request after each newly exposed level, so
the time loop drains the regrids one level at a time. Erase decisions need two
levels of agreement: the solver promotes a cell request to its parent only
after every child votes to erase, and the tree removes a parent flag only when
the complete adjacent cell ring agrees.

# Parallel behaviour

The refinement and ownership plans are replicated, while each rank
materialises only the levels above the spawn level, its own blocks and one
ring of neighbours. Each rank observes the requests of its local cells, so
the collective request exchange is required for a distributed run.

Repartitioning is part of the regrid. The target plan supplies the cell
weights of the deterministic rank partition, and the migration exchange moves
the cell slots whose ownership changes.

# Performance considerations

Plan evaluation scales with the request count and performs lattice lookups
without scanning the rank-local entity arrays. A regrid costs the rebuild of
the tree and the partition of the rank plus the transfer of the changed cells.
Stable criteria stop emitting requests once the standing plan satisfies them,
and erase criteria avoid alternating with protective refine requests.

*/
